00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.310 align:start position:0%
 
Warhawk  so you want to be a Gwent master you're  probably thinking oh I've beaten every  single merchant in The Witcher 3 I don't  need to listen to this guy well things  are kind of different this time around  the basics are still intact but the  finer details have been changed so here  are my five tips on how to kick ass in  the Gwent beta this should be a  no-brainer for anyone who's played Gwent  in the witcher 3 or any card game for  that matter you won't be drawing a new  card each turn so don't play all your  big guns right out of the gate unlike  Gwent in the witcher 3 you do draw two  cards at the beginning of the second  round and one at the beginning of the  third round this can act as a cushion  but don't rely on it chances are you  probably won't get the exact cards you  need by the rate of more halt planning  ahead also means that sometimes you  might have to throw around if you in the  first round don't overwork yourself to  win the second the last thing you want  is to be low on resources during that  final critical round I say you should  stay on pace with your opponent so he or  she burns through resources but don't go  all-in unless you are certain you can  win  in the tutorial it says to play your  hero cards early on but I say no any  last words  bluffing plays a huge role in Gwent and  if you play Geralt at the beginning your  opponent is going to know that one  you have Geralt in your deck and two  you've already used him instead I like  to play weaker cards and cards that  don't have any special abilities at the  beginning just to see how my opponent  plays I save Geralt for the very end if  it's coming down to the wire on that  third round and you play Geralt that's  basically a wind condition if an  opponent plays a hero card right at the  beginning in the first round I usually  start playing defensively and in most  cases I give that round to my opponent  because now I know Geralt is no longer a  problem  once again if you've played card games  then you know that it's better to keep  your deck on the leaner side there's no  point in filling your deck with cards  that could come in handy in very  specific situations instead look for  cards that serve multiple purposes and  build on your overall strategy a six  strength card like this one that gets +1  each turn in the fog isn't the best card  to have if you aren't focusing on  weather effects strategies sure a six  strength is decent but you'd be better  to find a card that fits your play style  this dip is kind of dependent on which  deck you choose the main but when I  place Galaga or northern realms I try to  pick cards for two rows and avoid cards  from the third then I pick whether cards  accordingly for example when I play  Northern realms I load my deck with  siege and infantry units and thrown a  couple fog cards  this way I can significantly weaken one  of my opponents lanes and because I  don't have many mid-range cards I should  come out unscathed  I tend to use my leader as a guide when  I'm building a deck unfortunately you  only start with one leader for each  faction at the beginning of the beta but  there are still plenty of good  strategies to build off of them  if Skellig ax is more your style than  I'd focus on cars that increase in  strength when cards on your side of the  battlefields have been weakened the  leader ability is to place to plan on  crate warriors when you place this card  it automatically loses to strength this  sounds like an awful ability at first  but if you play this with some other  cards it can be deadly for example the  clam tourists each x-men games to  strength for each unit on your side of  the battlefield that has its strengths  reduced but isn't destroyed you can take  things further with a clan broke VAR  Archer he deals one damage to three  units if you hit your own cards with  that then our clan Axman gets an extra  six strength but we aren't done yet or  cry doubles the strength of all your  units that have had their strength  reduced  now you're looking at a pretty deadly  board state and that's just one of the  many examples we saw obviously this is  still a beta so things could change but  in the meantime I hope this helps and if  you have any other tips please leave  them in the comments below or if you  want to take me on at Gwen find me on  Twitter at Jacob deck for more on all  things gaming in Witcher hit that  subscribe button

